{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails because it conflates 'rational preferability' with 'verisimilitude' in a way that secretly reintroduces induction. Popper explicitly rejected verisimilitude as a quantitative or comparative measure we can actually assess (following the Tichý and Miller proofs in the 1970s), yet the target claims we can choose the theory with the 'highest verisimilitude.' To assert that a better-corroborated theory has higher verisimilitude is to assume that past success in falsification tests correlates with closeness to truth in untested areas. This correlation is an inductive assumption about the uniformity of nature (that the mechanisms which allowed survival in the past will continue to reflect truth in the future). Therefore, the target does not solve the problem on Popper's terms but rather ignores Popper's own later admissions about the undefinability of verisimilitude and smuggles in the very inductive inference (past performance predicts truth-likeness) that the prompt asks to avoid.","premise":"Popper abandoned the idea that we can compare the verisimilitude of two false theories because such comparisons rely on inductive assumptions about unobserved content.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be assessed without induction, what non-inductive criterion does Popper actually offer for preferring one unfalsified theory over another in practice?","target":"7c3e5df7e79371b95e3b869934f14f25beed9d7611cfc0e6537f9696879e68b1"}]}